<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Assembly on cesun.life</title><link>https://example.org/tags/assembly/</link><description>Recent content in Assembly on cesun.life</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 13 Jan 2025 21:14:39 +0800</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://example.org/tags/assembly/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>NASM for x86 and x86-64</title><link>https://example.org/nasm/</link><pubDate>Mon, 13 Jan 2025 21:14:39 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/nasm/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;NASM translates Intel-syntax x86 assembly into a flat binary or an object file.
A flat binary contains emitted bytes with no standard relocation or symbol
metadata. An object file such as ELF, Mach-O, or COFF retains sections, symbols,
and relocations so a linker can combine it with other objects and libraries.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="sections-labels-and-symbols"&gt;
 Sections, Labels, and Symbols
 &lt;a href="#sections-labels-and-symbols" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;An object-file source normally separates executable code, initialized data, and
zero-initialized storage:&lt;/p&gt;


&lt;div class="jose-wrapper"&gt;
 &lt;div class="jose-tools"&gt; &lt;span&gt;nasm&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;button&gt;Copy&lt;/button&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;

 &lt;div class="jose-inner"&gt;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-nasm" data-lang="nasm"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;global&lt;/span&gt; add_one
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;extern&lt;/span&gt; external_value
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; .text
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;add_one:
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;mov&lt;/span&gt; eax, edi
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;add&lt;/span&gt; eax, &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#a6e22e"&gt;ret&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; .rodata
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;message: &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;hello&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;message_size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt; equ&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; message
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; .bss
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;scratch: &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;resq&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;A label defines the current assembly position. &lt;code&gt;GLOBAL&lt;/code&gt; exports a symbol to the
linker, while &lt;code&gt;EXTERN&lt;/code&gt; declares a symbol that another object must define. Local
labels beginning with &lt;code&gt;.&lt;/code&gt; are scoped to the preceding non-local label. Section
names and attributes depend on the output format; &lt;code&gt;.text&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.rodata&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.data&lt;/code&gt;,
and &lt;code&gt;.bss&lt;/code&gt; are ELF conventions rather than universal NASM keywords.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>x86 Architectural Registers</title><link>https://example.org/x86-registers/</link><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jul 2024 09:42:04 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/x86-registers/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;x86 architectural registers hold programmer-visible integer, control, status,
address-translation, debug, floating-point, and vector state. Width and
availability depend on execution mode and enabled extensions. Calling
conventions assign software roles to a subset of these registers but do not
change their architectural behavior.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="general-purpose-registers-and-subregisters"&gt;
 General-Purpose Registers and Subregisters
 &lt;a href="#general-purpose-registers-and-subregisters" class="heading-anchor"&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;#&lt;/span&gt;
 &lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;IA-32 exposes &lt;code&gt;EAX&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;EBX&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ECX&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;EDX&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ESI&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;EDI&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;EBP&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;ESP&lt;/code&gt;.
x86-64 widens them to &lt;code&gt;RAX&lt;/code&gt; through &lt;code&gt;RSP&lt;/code&gt; and adds &lt;code&gt;R8&lt;/code&gt; through &lt;code&gt;R15&lt;/code&gt;.
Subregisters name parts of the same architectural register:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>x86 and x86-64 Instruction Encoding</title><link>https://example.org/x86-instruction-encoding/</link><pubDate>Fri, 22 Apr 2022 00:33:12 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://example.org/x86-instruction-encoding/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;x86 uses variable-length instructions. An instruction can contain legacy prefixes,
